// ONE DECLARATION REACHED BY TWO ROUTES the module never wrote twice.
//
// A name a wildcard, an 'extends' or the implicit core import brings -- one the
// module never wrote -- is one binding with another of the same declaration
// under that name: the second is the binding the name has already [Jon 23 Sep].
// c declares x; a and b each re-export it; d wildcard-imports both, so x
// reaches d twice, and it is one name meaning one thing. Before the rule, d was
// refused at its second import, 'x is already a name of this module'.
//
// One side unwritten is enough. l lists x from a and takes b's wildcard, m does
// the same the other way round, and o writes 'use Option;' where the core
// import's wildcard has brought Some and None already. A name the module WRITES
// twice is refused (module-fold-written-nameres).
//
// Where one route is public and the other private, the binding is public, so a
// re-export is not lost to whichever route folded first. p and q write the same
// two imports in opposite orders, and each re-exports x either way.
//
// The folder holds:
//   c/c.cone   declares x
//   a/a.cone   re-exports c's names
//   b/b.cone   re-exports c's names
//   d/d.cone   wildcard-imports a and b, privately
//   l/l.cone   lists x from a, then wildcard-imports b
//   m/m.cone   wildcard-imports b, then lists x from a
//   o/o.cone   'use Option;' beside the core import's Some and None
//   p/p.cone   imports a privately, then b with 'pub use'
//   q/q.cone   imports b with 'pub use', then a privately

import stdio use *;

fn show(label &[]u8, n i64) {
  printStr(label);
  printStr(" = ");
  printInt(n);
  printStr("\n");
}

fn main() i32 {
  show("diamond", d.y());
  // c's x is 1 by either route
  show("listed-then-wildcard", l.lx());
  show("wildcard-then-listed", m.mx());
  show("written-enum-use-beside-core", o.oo());
  show("public-second", p.x());
  show("public-first", q.x());
  0i32;
}
